.TH DIFTRAYWM-KEYMAP 5
.SH NAME
keymap.ini - key remapping and key bindings for DiftrayWM
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every key in DiftrayWM is configured by an INI file in the format keyd uses. The
compositor's own configuration does not carry key settings; it names this file
and nothing more:
.PP
.RS
[general]
keymap = keymap.ini
.RE
.PP
The path is resolved relative to the configuration file, so a configuration
directory stays self-contained. Remove the setting, or point it at a file that
does not exist, and the compositor falls back to its built-in keys rather than
refusing to start.
.PP
The format is:
.PP
.RS
[section]
chord = Action(argument)
.RE
.PP
An unknown section, chord or action is a hard error reported with a line
number, not a silently ignored line, so a typo appears as a message instead of
a key that quietly does nothing.
.SH CHORDS
A chord is a key plus exactly the modifiers written.
.TP
.B <C-q>
Ctrl+q. Modifier prefixes are C for Ctrl, A for Alt, S for Shift, M for Meta
(the Logo/Super key) and G for Logo. They combine: <C-S-q> is Ctrl+Shift+q.
A chord means the modifiers written and no others, so <C-q> is not Ctrl+Shift+q
and not plain q.
.TP
.B <f8>
F1 to F12, written in lower case.
.TP
.B <space> <enter> <tab> <backspace> <esc> <del> <ins> <home> <end>
.TP
.B <up> <down> <left> <right> <pageup> <pagedown> <menu> <prtsc> <pause>
.TP
.B <grave> <minus> <equal> <leftbrace> <rightbrace> <backslash>
.TP
.B <semicolon> <apostrophe> <comma> <dot> <slash>
.TP
.B a-z, 0-9 and the punctuation keys
May be written bare. The character itself works too, so <;> and ; name the same
key.
.TP
.B <leftctrl> <rightctrl> <leftalt> <rightalt> <leftshift> <rightshift> <leftlogo> <rightlogo>
The physical modifier keys. The kernel calls the Logo keys LEFTMETA and
RIGHTMETA; they are spelled logo here because that is what the key is labelled.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B Diftray(command)
Dispatch a Command Bar command. Everything that can be typed after : works
here, which is what lets a profile drive the compositor without a new binary.
See diftraywm-commands(7).
.TP
.B Exec(command)
Run a program, detached, with WAYLAND_DISPLAY set to this session.
.TP
.B Typeout(text)
Type text into the focused cell's terminal. Refused when no cell is focused.
.TP
.B Trigger(profile)
Switch to another profile. The key is swallowed, so switching profile does not
also type the character.
.TP
.B Remap(<chord>)
Send a different chord downstream instead of this one. A remap cycle
terminates rather than recursing.
.TP
.B Ignore()
Swallow the key entirely, which is how a key is disabled.
.SH SECTIONS
.TP
.B [devices]
The keyboards the system-wide remapper should intercept, as
.I VENDOR:PRODUCT
in hexadecimal, one per line:
.PP
.RS
[devices]
keyboard = 320f:5080
.RE
.PP
Omit the section entirely to mean every keyboard, which is the safe default: a
machine that is only half remapped is a machine where the same chord does two
different things depending on which board it was typed on.
.TP
.B [init]
The Meta prefix.
.B prefix
is the chord, and
.B action
is what it does, which is normally Trigger() into a profile:
.PP
.RS
[init]
prefix = <C-q>
action = Trigger(profile-1)
.RE
.PP
Two further settings are accepted. default_profile names the profile active at
startup, defaulting to "default". system_wide is true or false, and is a
request rather than a switch: it asks for the system-wide backend as well, but
diftrayremap still has to be able to open /dev/uinput and still has to be
started deliberately.
.TP
.B [diftray]
The help pager's keys, which used to be settings in the configuration file:
help_key_close, help_key_search, help_key_next, help_key_previous,
help_key_page_down, help_key_page_up, help_key_line_down and help_key_line_up.
Each takes a single character or one of space, pagedown, pageup, up, down. The
values in the shipped keymap.ini are the defaults, so the whole section can be
deleted without changing behaviour.
.TP
.B [default] and any other name
A profile: a set of chord to action bindings. "default" is active at startup,
and the shipped keymap.ini documents the compositor's built-in keys there as
commented-out lines.
.SH THE PREFIX
The [init] prefix works the way keyd's does. Pressing it on its own runs the
[init] action, normally Trigger() into another profile, and the keypress is
swallowed so the chord never reaches the terminal. Bind a chord in the new
profile back to "default" to return to the normal keys. keymap reset and
diftrayctl keymap-reset do the same thing from outside, so nothing is lost if
no exit is bound.
.SH TWO BACKENDS, ONE FILE
The compositor applies this file to the keys it already reads. That needs no
privileges and affects only the DiftrayWM session.
.PP
diftrayremap(1) applies the same file system-wide. It takes the physical
keyboard away with EVIOCGRAB and republishes it through uinput, so the
remapping reaches every consumer on the machine: the compositor, a TTY,
another session, anything reading evdev. It needs access to /dev/input/event*
and /dev/uinput, which normally means running it as root, which is why it is a
separate program rather than a mode of the compositor. A compositor that
grabbed the keyboard would take it away from the session it is nested inside,
and would require every user to run their window manager as root.
.PP
Only evdev-level actions mean anything to diftrayremap. Ignore(), Remap() and
Trigger() work at that layer. Diftray(), Exec() and Typeout() are compositor
actions with no evdev equivalent, so those keys pass through untouched and the
compositor's own copy of this file handles them.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B :keymap show
Print the loaded keymap, its profiles, the active one and every binding.
.TP
.B :keymap reload
Re-read the file. A parse failure is reported and the previous keymap is kept.
.TP
.B :keymap profile <name>
Switch to another profile. keymap reset returns to the default profile.
.TP
.B :keymap path
Print the loaded keymap's path.
.TP
.B :keymap check <path>
Parse a candidate file and report what it contains, without adopting it. This
is how to diagnose an edit before reloading it.
.TP
.B :keymap chord <spec>
Validate a chord spelling and report the key code it resolves to, without
needing a file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DIFTRAYWM_KEYMAP
The file diftrayremap reads when given no argument. Defaults to keymap.ini.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I keymap.ini
The example keymap, installed next to diftray.conf.
.SH SEE ALSO
diftrayremap(1), diftrayctl(1), diftraywm-commands(7)
